# PLAN — etude-nad (b): make `etude capture` distinguish create from append

## Bead

**etude-nad** (P1 bug) — run refs under `refs/etude/runs/` have twice disappeared
mid-session; `etude capture` then silently rebuilt a fresh manifest, destroying
three recorded gate attempts while still printing `captured <sha>`.

Acceptance criteria (verbatim):

> The disappearance is root-caused or ruled out; and `etude capture` cannot
> silently discard an existing run's history — either it distinguishes create
> from append explicitly, or it fails loudly when a run it was expected to
> extend is missing.

This plan covers the **second clause only** — the guard, which the manager
directed to land first as its own commit. The first clause (root cause) is
already established and is summarised below because it justifies one design
decision; its remediation is a separate bead.

## (a) Root cause — established, with a reproduction

`etude init` registers a **fetch** refspec that maps the remote's etude
namespace onto the *same local namespace etude writes to*
(`internal/cli/init.go:198`, documented at `docs/init.md:30`):

```
remote.origin.fetch = +refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*
remote.origin.push  =  refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*
```

Per `git-fetch(1)`, refs fetched **due to an explicit configured refspec are
subject to pruning**. So every local `refs/etude/runs/<id>` is, to git, a
remote-tracking ref — and any `git fetch --prune` anywhere in the repository
**deletes every local run ref that is not yet on origin**.

Reproduced deterministically in a scratch repo (git 2.47.1):

```
--- before ---
22cc9c3 commit  refs/etude/runs/sentinel
--- git fetch --prune ---
From ../origin
 - [deleted]         (none)     -> refs/etude/runs/sentinel
--- after ---
(empty)
```

Trigger present in this environment: `workmux remove --gone` runs
`git fetch --prune` automatically (workmux's own docs: "`--gone`: Remove
worktrees whose upstream remote branch has been deleted (e.g., after a PR is
merged on GitHub). **Automatically runs `git fetch --prune` first.**"). The
etude-9uf work ran in a linked worktree, so a sibling lane's `workmux remove
--gone` operated on the shared common git dir and took every unpushed run ref
in the repository with it.

This explains every recorded observation: not reproducible on demand (nothing
in `go test`, `make test/lint/docs-check`, the walkthrough, `git stash`, or
`bd list` runs `fetch --prune`); refs present then absent "minutes later";
only *unpushed* runs affected (`dogfood-capture.sh` pushes at the very end);
and incident 2's fresh manifest, because a deleted ref makes the next
`etude capture` take the create branch.

Remediation is a separate change (stop registering the etude namespace as a
*fetch* refspec — `etude sync` already passes it explicitly on the command
line, `internal/cli/sync.go:94`, so the config entry buys nothing and costs
this). Filed as its own bead; out of scope here.

## The defect this plan fixes

`internal/cli/capture.go:145` resolves the run ref and branches:

```go
commit, resolveErr := r.store.Resolve(ctx, ref)
...
if resolveErr == nil { appending = true; ...merge prior stages/files... }
else if !errors.Is(resolveErr, refstore.ErrNotFound) { return resolveErr }
```

`ErrNotFound` therefore falls through to **create**, and both branches print the
identical `captured <sha>` / `ref <ref>`. A caller that *believes* the run exists
has no way to say so and no way to tell what happened.

The two sibling commands are already explicit, which makes `capture` the
inconsistent one:

| command | semantics on ref conflict | behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| `etude capture-run` | create-only | errors: *"run %q already exists … (create-only, will not clobber)"* (`capture_run.go:246`) |
| `etude capture-gate` | append-only | errors: *"run %q not found; a gate must attach to an existing run"* (`capture.go:432`) |
| `etude capture` | **ambiguous** | silently does either |

## Design

Add one flag to `etude capture`:

```
--expect create|append
```

- **`--expect append`** — the run ref MUST already exist. If it does not,
  fail before writing anything, non-zero, with a message that names the ref and
  says the history may have been lost rather than inviting a blind re-capture.
- **`--expect create`** — the run ref must NOT exist. If it does, fail before
  writing anything, non-zero, naming the ref and its current tip.
- **omitted** — today's auto behaviour, unchanged. Backward compatible.

An unrecognised value is a usage error naming the two accepted values.

Success output gains a third line naming the action taken:

```
captured <sha>
ref refs/etude/runs/<id>
action create        # or: action append
```

so that even a caller which passes no flag can *see* a create where it expected
an append. That line is what satisfies "distinguishes create from append
explicitly"; `--expect` is what satisfies "fails loudly".

Both checks are made against the single `Resolve` result already computed — no
extra git call, no second resolve, so no TOCTOU window is introduced beyond the
one `WriteCommit` already closes (create writes with `ExpectedOld: ""`, which
git enforces as "must not exist"; append writes with `ExpectedOld: <tip>`, a
CAS). `--expect` is a *fail-fast* check layered on top of those existing atomic
guarantees, not a replacement for them: if the ref is deleted between the
`Resolve` and the write, `--expect append` still cannot silently create,
because the write carries `ExpectedOld` and git fails it.

### Scope

`internal/cli/capture.go` (flag registration, `captureConfig`, `run`) plus
`internal/cli/capture_test.go`, `docs/capture.md`, and the generated
`docs/cli/etude_capture.md`. No change to `internal/refstore`, to
`capture-run`, or to `capture-gate` — those are already explicit.

### Rejected: enabling a reflog for `refs/etude/*`

The kickoff asked to consider landing `core.logAllRefUpdates` for these refs so
the next incident is diagnosable. **Tested, and it does not work for this
failure mode.** Measured in a scratch repo:

- `git -c core.logAllRefUpdates=always update-ref refs/etude/runs/sentinel …`
  does create `.git/logs/refs/etude/runs/sentinel`, and **subsequent plain git
  writes keep appending to it** (git logs a ref whose reflog file already
  exists regardless of the setting) — so a one-time creation at etude's own
  write would be enough to enable it permanently. That part works.
- But `git fetch --prune`'s deletion **unlinks the reflog together with the
  ref**. After the prune, `.git/logs/refs/etude/runs/` does not exist and
  `git reflog refs/etude/runs/sentinel` reports an unknown revision.

So it would have left exactly nothing behind for either recorded incident, and
nothing for the next one. It is cost without the forensic benefit it was
proposed for. Dropped deliberately, with the root cause established by other
means. (A `reference-transaction` hook *would* capture deletions, but that is a
repo-wide git hook, not this bead, and is moot now that the deleter is known.)

## Behaviour preservation

- The no-`--expect` path is unchanged except for the additional third stdout
  line. The existing `captured <sha>` and `ref <ref>` lines keep their format
  and their position (lines 1 and 2).
- Consumers checked: `internal/cli/run_test.go:769` and
  `internal/liverun/engine_test.go:134` assert `captured `-prefixed lines, but
  against the **liverun engine's** output (`internal/liverun/engine.go:452`), a
  different code path this bead does not touch. `scripts/dogfood-capture.sh`
  invokes `capture-run`, not `capture`, and does not parse its stdout.
- Append-conflict errors (`run_id` mismatch, `--workflow` /
  `--workflow-version` conflicts), artifact merging, ref merging, and the
  `ExpectedOld` CAS are untouched.
- No manifest/schema change, so no migration and no compatibility break in the
  stored format.

## Verification

- Planned regression: TestCaptureExpectAppendFailsWhenRunMissing — asserts
  non-zero error, the error names the run, and **no ref is created**.
- Planned regression: TestCaptureExpectCreateFailsWhenRunExists — asserts
  non-zero error and that the existing ref tip is **unchanged**.
- Planned regression: TestCaptureExpectAppendPreservesPriorStages — the real
  data-loss case: capture a stage, capture a second with `--expect append`,
  assert both stages and both artifacts survive.
- Planned regression: TestCaptureExpectCreateSucceedsOnFreshRun.
- Planned regression: TestCaptureRejectsUnknownExpectValue — usage error, no ref.
- Planned regression: TestCaptureReportsCreateAndAppendAction — stdout carries
  `action create` then `action append` across two captures.
- `make test` and `make lint` green.
- Manual CLI verification against a **built binary** in a scratch repo (this is
  a CLI-facing change): every `--expect` value, both success and both failure
  paths, and the exit codes.

## Docs

`docs/capture.md` gains `--expect` under Metadata/Appending, plus a short note
that a missing run ref is a data-loss signal rather than a fresh start.
`docs/cli/etude_capture.md` is generated — regenerate with `make docs` and run
`make docs-check`.
